</p><p id="Par2">This article [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>] has been retracted by the authors after discovering that a set of markers which would not pass quality control criteria has been used for the association study described, resulting in false positives being included. As a result, some sections of the article are inaccurate. To avoid confusion this article has been retracted and the authors have been given the opportunity to resubmit their corrected data. All authors agree to this retraction.</p></body><back><fn-group><fn><p>The original article can be found online at 10.1186/s12863-018-0620-0</p></fn></fn-group><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>Reference</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name name-style="western"><surname>Cai</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Dissecting closely linked association signals in combination with the mammalian phenotype database can identify candidate genes in dairy cattle</article-title><source>BMC Genet</source><year>2018</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>30</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/s12863-018-0620-0</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">29751743</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmcid">PMC5948690</pub-id><related-article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" related-article-type="retraction-forward" ext-link-type="pmc" xlink:href="PMC6288910"/></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article></pmc-articleset>